A subroutine activation record (also called a stack frame) typically contains

- the return address (C) – so that the caller can resume execution after the subroutine finishes;  
- the stack pointer (or a saved frame pointer) for the caller’s activation record (D), which lets the subroutine restore the previous stack frame when it returns;  
- space for the subroutine’s own local variables and any parameters (A).

It does **not** usually contain a heap area. Heap storage is managed separately and is not part of the stack‑based activation record.  

